the CUDA toolkit. CUDA++ is the MIT-licensed GPU half of Omega: a machine-code toolchain, runtime and driver client for AI inference on NVIDIA GB10 (sm_121), without the CUDA toolkit or NVIDIA user-space libraries. It is not a drop-in replacement for arbitrary CUDA applications. Try CUDA++ Read the source Understand inference speed What does replacing CUDA actually mean? CUDA is more than a compiler. A useful CUDA replacement has to account for the source language, machine-code generation, runtime, math libraries, driver interface and the physical GPU. Replacing one layer does not establish compatibility with all the others. CUDA++ takes a direct route: its own machine-code encoder, kernel builder, runtime and client for the open kernel module. On the qualified GB10 path there is no nvcc compilation, PTX pipeline, libcuda, cuBLAS or cuDNN at execution time. NVIDIA\'s kernel modules and firmware remain underneath it. The complete driver-client path on GitHub The CUDA++ replacement map CUDA layer CUDA++ path Boundary nvcc / PTX / ptxas Direct sm_121 machine-code encoder and kernel builder Qualified GB10 kernels; not a full CUDA C++ compiler libcuda Own client of the NVIDIA open kernel module Kernel-module interface must match CUDA runtime Sessions, memory, launches, command lists Bounded API; not arbitrary CUDA application compatibility cuBLAS / cuDNN Matrix multiply and attention for AIEN model paths Model-shaped coverage, not every operation or shape NVIDIA kernel driver Independent driver work is separate Not qualified as a replacement on real GB10 hardware Coverage, gaps and qualification bars on GitHub CUDA++, ROCm, SYCL, Vulkan, Metal and ZLUDA are different choices A CUDA alternative can mean a different GPU programming API, a vendor stack, a compatibility layer, or a new compiler and runtime. These are not interchangeable. ROCm is AMD\'s compute stack; SYCL is a cross-platform programming model; Vulkan compute is a graphics-and-compute API; Metal is Apple\'s API; ZLUDA targets CUDA compatibility. None of those names is evidence that an application works or is faster on your GPU. CUDA++\'s distinction is the inspectable path to machine code and its MIT licence. It is not a wrapper around ROCm, oneAPI or Metal. I am not claiming feature parity or a speed win over those projects. Choose by the operation you need, the chip that has actually run it, licence, deployment dependencies and a reproducible result. For the primary definitions:  ROCm ,  SYCL ,  Vulkan ,  Metal  and  ZLUDA . These are references, not CUDA++ dependencies. CUDA++ portability contract on GitHub Why this matters for machine learning and local AI Large language model inference repeatedly moves weights, multiplies matrices and updates attention state. Owning those kernels and their submission path makes memory traffic, launch overhead and scheduling visible. CUDA++ supplies the GPU side; Omega and the serving runtime provide the rest of AIEN\'s execution path. CUDA++ is not a complete machine-learning framework or a general training replacement. A captured 2026-10-21 process-library receipt records a Qwen3.6-35B-A3B FP8 server with zero CUDA libraries mapped. It also records the NVIDIA kernel modules and device files still in use. That is evidence for a bounded dependency claim, not a claim about the current server, every model, or comparative speed. Evidence on GitHub: the captured server, libraries and kernel driver Start with one real kernel Read the supported hardware and doctor output before running GPU work. Start with vector addition, verify every answer, then move to the model-shaped operations you need. Unsupported hardware should be a clear refusal, not a guess. git clone https://github.com/aien-dev/CUDApp\ncd CUDApp\nmake hello-gpu Setup and first-kernel instructions on GitHub CUDA++ implementation and evidence Read the inference performance guide Machine-readable CUDA++ dossier AIENOS Persistent machine intelligence on hardware you own. Experime